5.2.3 – Methodology Assumptions
- Product hierarchy mapping: The rationale explains the purpose of mapping but does not justify the appropriateness of the selected product classifications.
- Default values: The rationale focuses on completing missing attributes without addressing the potential distortion of cashflows caused by those defaults.
- Market curve mapping: The document does not explain the treatment of instruments whose reference index has no directly corresponding projection curve.
- Projection curve interpolation: It is unclear whether linear interpolation is performed on zero rates, discount factors or forward rates. These approaches can produce different cashflows.
- Classification of assumptions: Product mappings and day-count conventions are largely implementation settings, while the substantive assumptions underlying particular choices are not always identified.
5.2.4 – Limitations
- Source-system data availability: The stated mitigation focuses on reviewing input specifications, without addressing the impact of missing contractual attributes on generated cashflows.
- Compounded overnight rates: The limitation refers to an impact assessment but does not specify the scope or conditions under which the approximation remains immaterial.
- Material currencies: The limitation does not explicitly address the potential impact of assigning EUR projection curves to non-EUR/USD positions.
- Incomplete limitation inventory: The one-month CASA accrual approximation and omitted IRS payment delays are not reflected as limitations, despite potentially affecting the accuracy of the generated cashflows.
5.2.5 – Expert Judgement
- Governance: The section identifies where expert judgement is applied but does not clearly establish the approval and review arrangements for material judgements.
- Consistency: The statement that expert judgement is not applied during routine cashflow generation could be misleading, since production cashflows continue to depend on previously established judgement-based configurations.
